# ...except the headline run: it is the evidence behind the R@8 = 0.8976
# claim in the README, and it is what scripts/render_benchmark_shot.py
# renders the README screenshot from. ~90 KB of numbers, no documents.
!/tests/benchmarks/results/v0_k8.json

# ---------- Benchmark corpus (not in git) ----------
#
# The fixture corpus is ~49 MB of third-party documents — an Apple
# 10-K, an IRS W-2 form, arXiv papers, Wikipedia articles, a 32 MB
# scanned Arabic PDF. It is not committed:
#
#   * redistribution rights differ per document (the Wikipedia files
#     are CC-BY-SA and need attribution; the US-government ones are
#     public domain; the arXiv ones carry arXiv's non-exclusive
#     license), and tests/benchmarks/fixtures/README.md says to avoid
#     anything whose license does not permit redistribution "if you
#     intend to commit it";
#   * 49 MB of binaries in every clone of a library is a bad trade.
#
# The corpus is added to the repository separately once the provenance
# and attribution for every document are settled. Until then the
# parser benchmark and the fixture-dependent unit tests skip, and CI
# says so out loud rather than passing quietly.
#
# Ground truth (the queries and the expected pages) is small and
# reviewable — that is what actually belongs in the repository, and it
# lives in each fixture's ground_truth.json.
/tests/benchmarks/fixtures/
/tests/benchmarks/external_pdfs/

